(* ==========================================================================
   Butter VM — Instruction Set Architecture
   A stack-based virtual machine that is the compilation target for the
   Butter AST defined in GRAMMAR.bnf. Implemented in src/value.zig,
   src/chunk.zig, src/vm.zig, and src/compiler.zig.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. DESIGN GOALS ("RISC applied to a stack machine")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* a) FIXED-WIDTH INSTRUCTIONS. Every instruction is the same
      `struct { op: OpCode, operand: u32 }` (src/chunk.zig) regardless of
      `op`; an unused operand is simply zero. LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX (section
      3) pack two compile-time constants (an array's base slot and its
      length) into that one operand, 16 bits each, rather than growing
      Instruction a second field (`chunk.packIndexOperand`).

   b) LOAD/STORE ONLY. Arithmetic, comparison, and logical instructions take
      zero operands and work purely on values already on the stack; only the
      LOAD/STORE family (PUSH_CONST, LOAD_LOCAL, STORE_LOCAL, ...) names a
      location. READ/WRITE_BYTES (section 9) are the one exception — they
      move a whole run of bytes directly — but even they don't name slots
      themselves: they're handed an ARRAY_REF already produced by an
      ordinary LOAD/STORE-family instruction.

   c) ORTHOGONAL AND MINIMAL. Exactly one conditional branch (JUMP_IF_FALSE)
      and one unconditional one (JUMP); there is no JUMP_IF_TRUE because
      'or' is expressible as JUMP_IF_FALSE + JUMP (section 4).

   Every LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL operand is a slot index relative to the
   current call frame's base pointer, not absolute (sections 5-6). Top-level
   code is frame 0, with its base pointer fixed at 0 for the program's whole
   lifetime. There is no type-checking pass: the compiler resolves variable
   *names* to slots but never checks a variable's declared type against what
   it holds; the VM checks *runtime* value types operation-by-operation
   (section 3). *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. VALUE REPRESENTATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

<value>    ::= INT | FLOAT | BOOL | ARRAY_REF | STREAM | NULL | ENUM_TAG | OBJECT

(* INT and FLOAT are i64/f64.

   ARRAY_REF (GRAMMAR.bnf, arrays) is `{ base: u32, len: u32 }` — `base` an
   ABSOLUTE index into the VM's `stack` array (computed once, at
   MAKE_ARRAY_REF time, from whichever frame's `bp` the referenced array
   lives in — section 6), `len` its length. It is the runtime representation
   of a GENERIC (unsized, `int[]`-style) array parameter or return value
   ONLY — a fixed-size array never produces one. There is no Butter syntax
   that constructs an ARRAY_REF directly; the only source is MAKE_ARRAY_REF,
   compiler-generated wherever a fixed-size local is handed over by
   reference (a call argument matching a generic parameter, or an I/O
   buffer — section 9).

   STREAM (GRAMMAR.bnf, files) is `value.Stream`: `{ .standard: Standard }`
   naming stdin/stdout/stderr, or `{ .file: u32 }` naming a slot in the VM's
   own open-file table (section 10) — never a raw OS handle. A literal
   `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` keyword use compiles to a PUSH_CONST of a
   `.standard` stream constant; a `.file` stream is only ever produced by
   OPEN. A stream is trivially copyable and owns no resource itself —
   copying one copies only which stream it names.

   NULL is JSON's `null` — `value.Value`'s `null_value` variant, carrying no
   payload; there is exactly one null value.

   OBJECT is `*value.Object`, a refcounted heap allocation — the VM's only
   heap-managed value kind. It holds a STRING, a list, a map, or a RECORD
   (section 11 for the first three, section 19 for RECORD). Every string
   value is one of these: a compile-time literal, a map-literal key, a
   `MAP_KEYS` result, and a JSON-parsed string are all built the same way,
   via `Value.newString(allocator, bytes)` — there is no separate
   borrowed/unowned string representation. A Chunk's constant pool
   (`Chunk.constants`) holds one permanent owning reference to each
   OBJECT-backed constant, released on `Chunk.deinit`; PUSH_CONST increfs
   before pushing a copy onto the stack (section 11).

   ENUM_TAG (section 20) is `value.EnumTag`: `{ type_index: u32, variant:
   u32, type_name: []const u8, variant_name: []const u8 }` — a plain scalar,
   NOT an OBJECT (no heap allocation, no refcount; as cheap to copy as an
   INT). `type_index`/`variant` are what equality actually compares;
   `type_name`/`variant_name` are carried alongside purely so this value can
   describe itself when printed, with no side table to consult. Only ever
   produced by PUSH_CONST — an enum variant reference (`EnumName.Variant`)
   is a compile-time constant exactly like an INT or STRING literal, so
   there is no dedicated opcode for constructing one.

   A compiled program is one main Chunk (top-level code, run as an implicit
   frame 0 — section 6) plus one Chunk per declared function, each with its
   own constant pool; nothing is shared between them, since a function body
   can only ever reference its own parameters/locals (section 5).

   Arrays are NOT a `<value>` variant: a fixed-size array is simply
   `<declared size>` consecutive stack slots (section 5), each holding one
   plain scalar `<value>`. `len(arr)` on such a local compiles straight to
   PUSH_CONST of its declared size (a compile-time fold, `Local.array`'s
   `.fixed` payload — section 5); on a GENERIC local it compiles to
   LOAD_REF_LEN, a runtime read of the reference's own `len` field, since
   there's no compile-time number to fold to. *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. INSTRUCTION SET
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* Stack effect notation: ( before -- after ), stack grows to the right. *)

   PUSH_CONST   #idx     ( -- v )            increfs and pushes constants[idx]
   PUSH_TRUE              ( -- true )
   PUSH_FALSE             ( -- false )
   POP                    ( v -- )            decrefs and discards v

   LOAD_LOCAL   #slot     ( -- v )            increfs and pushes stack[slot]
   STORE_LOCAL  #slot     ( v -- v )          stack[slot] = v (peeks, does not
                                               consume — this is what lets
                                               'x := 5' be used as an
                                               expression); increfs v, then
                                               decrefs the old slot value

   LOAD_INDEX   #packed(slot,length)  ( index -- v )
                                               pops index (must be INT;
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise,
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= index < length); pushes
                                               stack[slot + index], increffed
   STORE_INDEX  #packed(slot,length)  ( index v -- v )
                                               same checks as LOAD_INDEX;
                                               sets stack[slot + index] = v
                                               (increfs v, decrefs the old
                                               value), pushes v back

   MAKE_ARRAY_REF #packed(slot,length)  ( -- ref )
                                               pushes ARRAY_REF{ base = bp +
                                               slot, len = length }. Only
                                               ever emitted when passing a
                                               fixed-size local into a
                                               generic (`[]`) parameter or
                                               I/O buffer position.
   LOAD_INDEX_REF #slot   ( index -- v )      pops index (must be INT); reads
                                               ref = stack[bp+slot] (must be
                                               ARRAY_REF); IndexOutOfBounds
                                               unless 0 <= index < ref.len;
                                               pushes stack[ref.base+index],
                                               increffed. The generic-array
                                               counterpart to LOAD_INDEX:
                                               `slot` names where the
                                               REFERENCE lives, not the array.
   STORE_INDEX_REF #slot  ( index v -- v )    generic-array counterpart to
                                               STORE_INDEX.
   LOAD_REF_LEN  #slot    ( -- int )          reads ref = stack[bp+slot]
                                               (must be ARRAY_REF); pushes
                                               ref.len as an INT.

   ADD                    ( a b -- r )        two shapes: both operands
                                               numeric (INT or FLOAT) behaves
                                               like SUB/MUL/etc. below; both
                                               operands STRING instead pushes
                                               a FRESH heap string (section
                                               11) of a's bytes followed by
                                               b's, decreffing both operand
                                               objects (their bytes are
                                               copied, not adopted). Any other
                                               pairing (including one STRING
                                               and one numeric) is
                                               TypeMismatch, decreffing
                                               whichever operand(s) are heap
                                               objects.
   SUB, MUL, DIV, MOD, POW
                          ( a b -- r )        both operands numeric (INT or
                                               FLOAT); INT op INT stays INT
                                               (overflow-checked); any FLOAT
                                               operand promotes both. DIV/MOD
                                               by zero and INT overflow are
                                               runtime errors, not traps.
                                               Decref both operands on a
                                               TypeMismatch path.

   NEG                    ( a -- r )          numeric only
   NOT                    ( a -- r )          BOOL only

   EQ, NEQ                ( a b -- bool )     defined for every pair of value
                                               types: numeric operands compare
                                               across INT/FLOAT by value;
                                               string-shaped values compare
                                               by content; a list/map compares
                                               by POINTER IDENTITY, never
                                               deep; any other mismatched pair
                                               of types simply compares
                                               unequal. Decrefs both operands
                                               after comparing.
   LT, LTE, GT, GTE        ( a b -- bool )    two shapes, mirroring ADD: both
                                               operands numeric compare by
                                               value; both operands STRING
                                               compare byte-lexicographically
                                               (an equal common prefix orders
                                               the shorter one first),
                                               decreffing both operand
                                               objects. Any other pairing is
                                               TypeMismatch (decreffing
                                               whichever operand(s) are heap
                                               objects).

   JUMP         #target               ip := target
   JUMP_IF_FALSE #target  ( bool -- bool )    peeks (does not pop) the
                                               condition; if false, ip :=
                                               target. Every use site is
                                               responsible for POPping the
                                               condition itself on both paths
                                               (section 4). TypeMismatch if
                                               the peeked value isn't BOOL.

   PUSH_HANDLER #target                       pushes a handler onto the
                                               handler stack (section 14),
                                               recording the current chunk,
                                               `target` (the catch block's
                                               entry offset in it), sp, bp,
                                               frame count and return width —
                                               everything needed to resume
                                               there later. No effect on the
                                               value stack.
                                               HandlerStackOverflow past 64
                                               live handlers, which is itself
                                               NOT catchable.
   POP_HANDLER                                discards the innermost handler.
                                               Emitted where a try body falls
                                               off its end normally; a RET
                                               out of the frame drops the
                                               frame's handlers on its own
                                               (section 14), so no other exit
                                               needs one.

   CALL         #func_index  ( arg1 ... argN -- result )
                                               the N arguments (N = the
                                               callee's arity) are already on
                                               the stack; CALL pushes nothing
                                               itself. Suspends the current
                                               chunk (saving ip and base
                                               pointer as a new frame —
                                               section 6) and jumps to
                                               functions[func_index]'s chunk
                                               at instruction 0, with the
                                               base pointer set so slot 0 is
                                               exactly where arg1 already
                                               sits. CallStackOverflow if the
                                               frame stack is already full.
   RET                     ( v -- v )          pops the current frame
                                               (decreffing every argument and
                                               local it owned — section 6),
                                               leaves v as the sole result on
                                               the caller's stack. Every
                                               function chunk is guaranteed by
                                               the compiler to end in a RET.
                                               Also drops every handler
                                               belonging to the departing
                                               frame (section 14) — a
                                               `return` out of a try block is
                                               what makes this necessary.

   PRINT                  ( v -- )            pops v, decrefs it, writes it
                                               followed by a newline, always
                                               to the `out` stream (section 9)

   PUSH_ARGS               ( -- list )        builds a FRESH list Object
                                               (section 11) from `Host.args`
                                               (vm.zig), one FRESH heap
                                               string per element, in order,
                                               and pushes it. What the bare
                                               `args` keyword (GRAMMAR.bnf
                                               design note 3p) compiles to.
                                               Unlike PUSH_CONST of a
                                               `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr`
                                               stream constant, this reads
                                               live Host state rather than
                                               the chunk's constant pool,
                                               since argv isn't known until
                                               `Vm.run` is called. `Host.args`
                                               defaults to an empty slice, so
                                               an embedder that never sets it
                                               gets an empty `args` list.

   READ         #stream   ( ref -- count )    pops an ARRAY_REF naming the
                                               destination buffer
                                               (TypeMismatch if it isn't one),
                                               reads at least 1 and at most
                                               ref.len bytes from `stream`,
                                               stores each as an INT in
                                               0..255 into stack[ref.base+i],
                                               pushes how many were read (0,
                                               and only 0, at end of input).
                                               A SHORT read, like POSIX
                                               read(2). StreamReadFailed if
                                               the underlying stream errors.
   WRITE        #stream   ( v -- count )      pops v, decrefs it, writes it
                                               to `stream` in exactly PRINT's
                                               rendering but with no trailing
                                               newline, pushes the byte
                                               count. StreamWriteFailed if the
                                               underlying stream errors.
   WRITE_BYTES  #stream   ( ref count -- written )
                                               pops count (must be INT), then
                                               an ARRAY_REF; the inverse of
                                               READ. IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= count <= ref.len,
                                               ByteOutOfRange if any of the
                                               first `count` elements isn't
                                               an INT in 0..255. Writes those
                                               bytes to `stream`, pushes how
                                               many were written.

   MAKE_LIST    #n        ( v1..vn -- list )  moves the top n values into a
                                               fresh list Object (a pure
                                               move — no incref/decref
                                               needed). #0 is what a bare
                                               `list xs` (no initializer), or
                                               a `list`-returning function
                                               falling off the end, compiles
                                               to.
   MAKE_MAP     #n        ( k1 v1 .. kn vn -- map )
                                               builds a fresh map Object from
                                               n key/value pairs in order (a
                                               repeated key is last-wins).
                                               TypeMismatch if a key isn't
                                               string-shaped.
   INDEX_GET               ( container index -- v )
                                               pops index, then container;
                                               dispatches on container's kind
                                               (list: index must be INT,
                                               bounds-checked, pushes the
                                               found element increffed
                                               (still owned by the list too);
                                               map: index must be
                                               string-shaped, KeyNotFound if
                                               absent, pushes the found value
                                               increffed (still owned by the
                                               map too); string: index must
                                               be INT, bounds-checked against
                                               the string's byte length,
                                               pushes a FRESH length-1 string
                                               (`Value.newString`) — already
                                               owned outright by this push,
                                               so unlike the list/map cases
                                               there is nothing else to
                                               incref against; else
                                               TypeMismatch). What bracket-
                                               indexing compiles to whenever
                                               the indexed thing isn't a bare
                                               local resolving to a fixed/
                                               generic array.
   INDEX_SLICE             ( container start end -- v )
                                               pops end, start, then
                                               container (start/end must be
                                               INT, else TypeMismatch);
                                               container must be a string,
                                               else TypeMismatch.
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= start <= end <= len(s).
                                               Pushes a fresh string
                                               (`Value.newString`) of the
                                               bytes `[start, end)` — end
                                               exclusive, `start == end`
                                               legal and yields `""`. What
                                               `s[a..b]` compiles to
                                               (GRAMMAR.bnf's Strings design
                                               notes); there is no slice
                                               counterpart for list/map in
                                               this pass. Read-only like
                                               INDEX_GET's string case: no
                                               STORE form exists.
   INDEX_SET               ( container index v -- v )
                                               same dispatch as INDEX_GET,
                                               mutating instead: a map
                                               insert-or-updates; a list
                                               requires an in-bounds index.
                                               Increfs v, decrefs whatever
                                               value it replaces, pushes v
                                               back (peek-shaped, like
                                               STORE_INDEX).
   LIST_PUSH               ( list v -- newlen )
                                               appends v to list (a pure
                                               move) and pushes its new
                                               length. TypeMismatch if the
                                               first operand isn't a list.
   MAP_HAS                 ( map key -- bool ) whether key is present, with
                                               no error for a missing one.
   MAP_DELETE               ( map key -- bool )
                                               removes key if present
                                               (freeing its owned key copy,
                                               decreffing its value); pushes
                                               whether it existed.
   MAP_KEYS                ( map -- list )     a fresh list of fresh
                                               string-value COPIES of map's
                                               own keys (never aliasing the
                                               map's own storage), in
                                               insertion order.
   LEN_VALUE                ( v -- int )       what the relaxed
                                               `len(<expression>)` compiles
                                               to whenever the argument isn't
                                               a bare fixed/generic-array
                                               identifier: list -> element
                                               count, map -> entry count,
                                               string -> byte length (ISA.bnf
                                               section 11's BYTE-indexed
                                               convention, not codepoints),
                                               else TypeMismatch.
   JSON_PARSE               ( ref count -- v )  gathers `count` bytes from
                                               the buffer ref points at
                                               (reusing WRITE_BYTES's own
                                               staging area) and parses them
                                               as JSON, pushing whatever the
                                               document's root turns out to
                                               be. JsonParseFailed (with a
                                               `Vm.diagnostic`) if the bytes
                                               aren't well-formed JSON.
   JSON_STRINGIFY           ( v -- string )    renders v as JSON text and
                                               pushes it as a fresh string
                                               value. TypeMismatch if v is
                                               (or contains, nested inside a
                                               list/map) a stream or an array
                                               reference — neither has a
                                               JSON representation.

   EXEC                     ( command args -- map )
                                               pops args (must be a list of
                                               strings; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise), then command (must
                                               be string-shaped; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise); spawns command with
                                               args as its own argv[1..],
                                               waits for it to exit, and
                                               pushes a fresh map with
                                               "stdout"/"stderr"/"exit_code".
                                               ProcessesUnavailable with no
                                               `Host.process`;
                                               ProcessSpawnFailed if command
                                               couldn't be started;
                                               ProcessTerminatedAbnormally if
                                               it didn't exit normally. Full
                                               detail in section 17. What
                                               `exec(command, args)`
                                               (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3x)
                                               compiles to.

   EXIT                     ( code -- )        pops code (must be INT;
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise;
                                               InvalidExitCode unless
                                               0 <= code <= 255); flushes and
                                               closes every open file exactly
                                               like HALT, decrefs the entire
                                               stack across every active
                                               frame (same as HALT — `exit`
                                               is reachable mid-function,
                                               several call frames deep), and
                                               stores code on `Vm.exit_code`
                                               before returning. What `exit
                                               <expression>` (GRAMMAR.bnf
                                               design note 3q) compiles to.
                                               Unlike every RuntimeError, this
                                               is a NORMAL return from `run`
                                               (`exit(0)` is not a failure) —
                                               the embedder distinguishes it
                                               from an ordinary HALT (which
                                               leaves `Vm.exit_code` null)
                                               purely by checking that field.

   MAKE_STRUCT  #type_index  ( f1..fn -- struct )
                                               n = struct_types[type_index]
                                               .field_names.len (never a
                                               separate operand — mirrors
                                               CALL not separately encoding
                                               arity); pops the n values
                                               already on the stack (a pure
                                               MOVE — no incref/decref, same
                                               as MAKE_LIST) and builds a
                                               fresh RECORD object from them,
                                               in order, sharing
                                               struct_types[type_index]'s own
                                               type_name/field_names slices;
                                               pushes it. Full detail in
                                               section 19. What a struct
                                               literal (`TypeName{...}`,
                                               GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3z)
                                               compiles to.
   FIELD_GET    #field_index  ( struct -- v )  pops struct (always a RECORD
                                               object — compiler-verified,
                                               never anything else reaches
                                               here from compiled source);
                                               pushes fields[field_index],
                                               increffed. Unlike INDEX_GET,
                                               this can raise no
                                               RuntimeError at all — the
                                               field's existence and index
                                               are both resolved at compile
                                               time (`SemanticError
                                               .UnknownField` otherwise).
                                               What `p.field` (a struct
                                               field read) compiles to.
   FIELD_SET    #field_index  ( struct v -- v )
                                               increfs v, decrefs the old
                                               fields[field_index], stores v
                                               there, decrefs struct (this
                                               instruction's own reference to
                                               it — the record itself lives
                                               on independent of this),
                                               pushes v back. Same "no
                                               RuntimeError possible" story
                                               as FIELD_GET. What `p.field :=
                                               v` compiles to.

   HALT                                       stop execution (only ever
                                               appears in the main chunk, at
                                               the very end); `Vm.exit_code`
                                               stays null, which the embedder
                                               treats as exit code 0


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. CODEGEN PATTERNS (backpatched-jump lowering of control flow and
      short-circuiting)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   if-stmt (condition, then, optional else):

       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       POP                          (* discard a truthy condition *)
       <then-branch>
       JUMP  end_target
     else_target:
       POP                          (* discard a falsy condition either way *)
       [ <else-branch> ]
     end_target:

   while-stmt:

     loop_start:
       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP

   try-stmt (body, error binding, handler — section 14):

       PUSH_HANDLER  catch_target
       <try body>                   (* its own scope; locals POPped at end *)
       POP_HANDLER
       JUMP  end_target
     catch_target:
       <catch body>                 (* the error struct is ALREADY on the
                                       stack, pushed by the VM while
                                       unwinding, and IS the binding's local
                                       slot *)
       POP                          (* discard the binding, via the normal
                                       end-of-scope pop *)
     end_target:

   The binding needs no STORE_LOCAL and no opcode of its own. At the `try`
   statement the compiler's next free slot is some N; the try body's own
   locals occupy N upward and are popped at its end, so the catch block is
   compiled with the next free slot back at N and declares the binding
   there, statically typed as the built-in `Error` struct (section 19). At
   run time a statement boundary always has sp == bp + N (locals below, no
   live temporaries), so the sp PUSH_HANDLER recorded is exactly the
   binding's address — unwinding restores that sp and then pushes the error
   struct, landing it precisely in slot N. The end-of-scope POP then
   discards it with no special case.

   'and' (short-circuits a false left operand without evaluating right):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  end_target
       POP                          (* left true -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:

   'or' (short-circuits a true left operand; built from JUMP + JUMP_IF_FALSE
   rather than a dedicated JUMP_IF_TRUE, per section 1c):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       JUMP  end_target             (* left true -> skip right *)
     else_target:
       POP                          (* left false -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. VARIABLE RESOLUTION (compile time, no runtime name lookup)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   The compiler keeps a list of (name, scope_depth, slot, width) entries in
   declaration order, separately per chunk being compiled (the main chunk,
   then each function's chunk). `slot` is tracked via a running `next_slot`
   counter rather than implied by list position, since `width` isn't always
   1: a scalar local occupies one slot, a fixed-size array local occupies
   `<declared size>` consecutive slots. Entering a <block> increases
   scope_depth; leaving it removes every local declared at that depth,
   decrements `next_slot` by each removed local's width, and emits one POP
   per slot removed. A reference to (or assignment to) an undeclared name is
   `CompileError.UndefinedVariable`. Shadowing an outer variable in an inner
   block is allowed (lookup walks the list from the end, innermost wins);
   redeclaring a name within one scope simply shadows the earlier slot.

   An array-typed local's bare name resolves for LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL
   exactly like a scalar's, but using it that way is
   `CompileError.ArrayUsedAsScalar` — a fixed-size array is only ever
   accessed through LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX. `Local.array` distinguishes
   `.fixed(n)` (width `n`, LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX) from `.generic` (width 1
   — a single ARRAY_REF value — LOAD_INDEX_REF/STORE_INDEX_REF/
   LOAD_REF_LEN). `Local.collection` is a separate, independent optional
   field (null / `.map` / `.list`, checked before `Local.array`) for a
   map/list local, which is always exactly one heap-reference slot,
   addressed via INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET rather than any array opcode.

   `Local.named_ref` (section 19/20) is a third, independent optional axis
   (set only when `Local.value_type == .named` — a struct- or enum-typed
   local/parameter) recording which registered type this is and whether
   it's a struct or an enum — never combined with `.array` (a struct/enum
   local is always exactly one slot, same as a map/list), stored separately
   from `Local.value_type` itself rather than turning it into a payload-
   carrying union, for the same reason `Local.collection`/`Local.is_string`
   are their own fields rather than folded into `Local.array`: it keeps
   every existing `Local.value_type == .int`-style comparison working
   unchanged. A struct-typed local's field reads/writes go through
   FIELD_GET/FIELD_SET (section 19), never LOAD_INDEX/INDEX_GET; an
   enum-typed local is an ordinary LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL scalar, exactly
   like an INT or BOOL local, just carrying an ENUM_TAG value instead.

   A function's parameters are its first locals, occupying slots
   0..arity-1: a scalar parameter costs one slot, a `.fixed(n)` array
   parameter costs `n`, a `.generic` array parameter costs exactly one (it's
   a single ARRAY_REF value). Each function's locals list is entirely
   separate and discarded once its chunk finishes compiling, which is what
   makes it structurally impossible for a function body to resolve a
   top-level or another function's variable.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. FUNCTION CALLS & RECURSION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   Each declared function compiles to its own `chunk_mod.Function` — name,
   arity, return_width, and a self-contained Chunk. A compiled `Program` is
   one main Chunk plus a slice of these, indexed by the operand CALL
   instructions use (resolved by name to index at compile time, before any
   function body is compiled, so calls resolve regardless of declaration
   order).

   ARITY AND RETURN_WIDTH ARE STACK-SLOT COUNTS, NOT VALUE COUNTS. `arity`
   is the total slots every argument occupies (a scalar is 1, a fixed-size
   array argument is its declared size); `return_width` is likewise how many
   slots the return value occupies. A call with one array-typed and one
   scalar parameter has an arity of `<array size> + 1`, not 2 — though the
   arity-MISMATCH check (GRAMMAR.bnf, functions) still counts declared
   PARAMETERS one-for-one with arguments, a different number from `arity`
   whenever any parameter is array-typed.

   CALLING CONVENTION. Arguments compile left to right onto the stack: a
   scalar argument is one ordinary expression; an array-typed argument is a
   sequence of LOAD_LOCALs copying that array's slots on top of the stack,
   always a fresh copy, never a reference to the caller's own slots. By the
   time CALL executes, the callee's entire `arity`-slot argument list is
   sitting on top of the stack in order. CALL then:

     1. Checks the frame stack isn't already full (CallStackOverflow
        otherwise).
     2. Pushes a frame recording the caller's chunk, ip (already past the
        CALL instruction), base pointer, and return_width.
     3. Sets the new base pointer to `sp - arity`, so the already-pushed
        arguments become exactly the callee's slots 0..arity-1 with no
        copying.
     4. Switches to the callee's chunk, jumps to instruction 0, and sets
        the active return_width to the callee's own.

   RET generalizes "pop the result, restore the frame, push it back" to
   `return_width` values at once: it decrefs every value in
   `stack[bp .. sp-return_width)` (the departing frame's own locals,
   arguments, temporaries), copies the top `return_width` slots down onto
   the frame's own base pointer in place, sets sp to `bp + return_width`,
   pops the frame, and restores the caller's chunk/ip/base pointer/
   return_width.

   RECURSION FALLS OUT FOR FREE: each call gets its own frame and base
   pointer into a fresh region of the same stack array, so a function
   calling itself (or two functions calling each other) needs no special
   handling — until either the frame stack (CallStackOverflow) or the value
   stack (StackOverflow) runs out of room. TOP LEVEL IS FRAME 0: it never
   gets a frame pushed for itself (nothing ever CALLs into it), which is why
   `return` is restricted to inside a function body — there is no frame at
   the top level for RET to pop.

   GENERIC ARRAY ARGUMENTS. A generic (`int[]`) parameter costs exactly 1
   slot, holding one ARRAY_REF. Passing a FIXED-size local emits
   MAKE_ARRAY_REF at the call site (`base = bp + <local's slot>`, using the
   CALLER's still-in-scope bp); passing an already-generic local simply
   forwards its existing reference via LOAD_LOCAL.

   WHY A REFERENCE IS SAFE AS AN ARGUMENT BUT NOT ALWAYS SAFE AS A RETURN. An
   argument's reference is only ever read during the callee's own call,
   always shorter-lived than the frame it points into. A `return` is
   different: the reference must remain valid after this function's own
   frame is discarded. One synthesized by THIS call's own MAKE_ARRAY_REF
   points into THIS frame, about to be popped; only a reference already
   received as a parameter (or forwarded from a nested call, transitively) —
   which by induction always points into some still-live ANCESTOR frame — is
   safe to return. The compiler enforces this statically
   (`compileGenericArrayReturn`, `CompileError.EscapingArrayReference`)
   rather than leaving it a runtime footgun.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. THE FOR-LOOP (desugars to section 4's while-stmt pattern)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `for v in start..end body` introduces a wrapper scope holding two hidden
   locals — the range's end bound (evaluated once) and the loop variable —
   then emits exactly the while-stmt pattern from section 4:

       <end-expr>                    (* hidden local: end_slot *)
       <start-expr>                  (* hidden local: var_slot, = v *)
     loop_start:
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       LOAD_LOCAL  end_slot
       LT
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>                        (* v resolves as an ordinary local *)
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       PUSH_CONST  #1
       ADD
       STORE_LOCAL  var_slot
       POP
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP
       (* both hidden locals popped as the wrapper scope closes, section 5 *)

   No new opcodes are involved. The hidden end-bound local is given the
   empty string as its name — never a legal IDENTIFIER lexeme — so it can
   never collide with a name the program actually writes.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. IMPORT/EXPORT (compile-time only — no new opcodes, no runtime concept
      of a "module")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   import/export add one compile-time concern — which names a given file may
   call — and touch nothing at runtime: CALL's operand is still a single
   flat index into one program-wide function table. Lives in src/module.zig
   and `compiler.Compiler.compileModules` (`compileProgram` is a thin
   wrapper handing it a single module with an empty import list).

   THE MODULE LOADER RUNS BEFORE COMPILATION. Given an entry program,
   `module.Loader` recursively lexes and parses every transitively imported
   file, depth-first, before any compilation begins. Each file is keyed by a
   normalized path (resolved relative to the *importing* file's directory)
   and memoized — a path already loaded is returned instead of re-read,
   which is the entire mechanism behind "a shared dependency is compiled
   exactly once": a diamond-shaped import graph collapses to one entry per
   distinct file. A cycle (A imports B imports A) is `LoadError.
   CircularImport`, caught via a stack of paths currently mid-load.

   FUNCTION REGISTRATION IS GLOBAL, VISIBILITY IS PER-MODULE.
   `compileModules`'s first pass registers every module's function
   declarations into one flat name table (name, arity, call-index, owning
   module, `exported` flag); a name collision anywhere in that table —
   even between files that don't import each other — is
   `SemanticError.DuplicateFunction`. Compiling a call to name `f` then
   checks visibility: it belongs to the current module (always allowed), or
   to a module in the current module's direct-import list AND is exported
   (`SemanticError.FunctionNotVisible` otherwise) — deliberately NOT
   transitive; importing B does not expose B's own imports.

   A BUNDLED STANDARD LIBRARY IS RESOLVED BY NAME, NOT PATH. Before an
   `import` path is resolved against the importing file's directory, it's
   checked against a small table of reserved `.std.butter` names (currently
   just `"math.std.butter"`, `src/stdlib.zig`) whose source is embedded into
   the `butter` binary at build time via `@embedFile`. A hit resolves to
   that embedded source directly, with no file needing to exist on disk;
   past that lookup it's loaded and compiled exactly like any other module.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   9. BYTE-STREAM I/O (READ, WRITE, WRITE_BYTES — section 3)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   THE STREAM IS A VALUE ON THE STACK, NOT AN OPERAND: all three instructions
   take zero operands and pop the STREAM they act on off the top of the
   stack — pushed there by whatever expression named it (a PUSH_CONST of a
   literal stream, or the result of OPEN) — rather than being told which
   stream via their operand. This is what lets a file's stream reach
   read/write without dedicated "it happens to be a file" instructions.

   DIRECTION IS CHECKED AT COMPILE TIME WHEN IT CAN BE, AT RUN TIME
   OTHERWISE. `compileStreamOperand` statically checks direction
   (`CompileError.WrongStreamDirection`) only for a literal
   `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` keyword use. For any other stream expression the
   VM checks instead: `readerFor`/`writerFor` (section 10) raise
   `RuntimeError.StreamNotReadable`/`StreamNotWritable` if the stream turns
   out to be opened in the wrong direction.

   THE BUFFER ARRIVES AS AN ARRAY_REF ON THE STACK, produced the same way a
   generic call argument is (MAKE_ARRAY_REF for a fixed-size local,
   LOAD_LOCAL for one that already holds a reference) — which is why there
   is no READ/READ_REF pair; by the time READ executes it can no longer tell
   which kind of array it was handed, and doesn't need to. A reference
   synthesized for an I/O buffer is trivially safe (section 6): it's created
   and fully consumed by a single instruction inside the very frame it
   points into.

   BYTES AND VALUES ARE TWO DIFFERENT TRANSLATIONS, both routed through
   `Vm.io_buffer`, a fixed `[stack_max]u8` staging area (large enough by
   construction to hold any buffer the value stack itself could contain):
     - READ reads into `io_buffer`, then scatters each byte out as an INT
       (`Vm.scatterBytes`).
     - WRITE_BYTES gathers each slot back into `io_buffer`, checking each
       one really names a byte (`Vm.gatherBytes`), then writes that flat
       slice once.
   WRITE (the value form) needs neither, since it's writing a rendering, not
   bytes: it counts the rendered length with a discarding pass, then renders
   again to the real stream.

   EVERYTHING OUTSIDE THE VM IS INJECTED. `Vm.run` takes a
   `Host { out, err, in, fs }`. `out` is required; `in`, `err`, and `fs` are
   each optional, and an absent one behaves like the shell redirection or
   permission it stands in for: a missing `in` makes every READ report 0, a
   missing `err` drops written bytes after counting them, and a missing `fs`
   fails every OPEN with `RuntimeError.FilesUnavailable`.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   10. FILES (OPEN, CLOSE — section 3; one new runtime table)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `open`/`close` add one piece of VM state: a fixed table of open files
   (`Vm.files`, `max_open_files = 8` slots of `?OpenFile`), part of the `Vm`
   struct itself. `Value.stream`'s `.file` payload is a `u32` INDEX into
   this table, never a raw OS handle — copying a stream value copies only
   which slot it names. The real resource (an `std.Io.File`, a buffered
   reader OR writer — a file is opened in exactly one direction — and its
   own byte buffer) lives in the table entry, owned by the `Vm`. A `null`
   slot is free; CLOSE always resets a slot to `null`, so using a closed
   file is a clean `RuntimeError.StreamClosed` rather than a stale handle.

   OPEN #mode   ( path -- stream )    pops path (must be string-shaped;
                                       TypeMismatch otherwise) and opens it
                                       against `Host.fs` in the given `mode`
                                       (`value.OpenMode` — the one operand,
                                       since the mode is always a bare
                                       keyword). On success, records the
                                       file in the first free table slot and
                                       pushes a STREAM naming it.
                                         - `RuntimeError.FilesUnavailable` if
                                           `Host.fs` is null.
                                         - `RuntimeError.TooManyOpenFiles` if
                                           every table slot is in use.
                                         - `RuntimeError.FileOpenFailed`
                                           otherwise (no such file in `read`
                                           mode, no permission, etc).
                                       All three set `Vm.diagnostic`
                                       (`operation = "open"`, `path`, and a
                                       `cause`). `write` mode truncates or
                                       creates; `read` mode requires the file
                                       to exist; `append` opens for writing
                                       and seeks to the current end (or
                                       creates it if absent).

   CLOSE                    ( stream -- )   pops a STREAM (TypeMismatch if
                                       it isn't one).
                                         - A `.standard` stream is
                                           `RuntimeError.
                                           CannotCloseStandardStream` (the
                                           compiler already rejects a
                                           literal case at compile time).
                                         - A `.file` naming a slot that
                                           isn't currently open is
                                           `RuntimeError.StreamClosed`.
                                         - Otherwise: flushes the file if
                                           opened for writing
                                           (`RuntimeError.FileCloseFailed`
                                           if the flush fails), closes the
                                           underlying OS file, and frees the
                                           table slot UNCONDITIONALLY either
                                           way.

   READ/WRITE/WRITE_BYTES (section 9) work on a file stream unchanged,
   dispatching through `Vm.readerFor`/`writerFor` (`Vm.fileEntry` looks up
   the table entry and returns whichever half of its `Handle` exists):
   reading a stream opened `write`/`append` (or writing to one opened
   `read`) is `RuntimeError.StreamNotReadable`/`StreamNotWritable`; a closed
   or invalid slot is `RuntimeError.StreamClosed`.

   EVERY OPEN FILE IS CLOSED ON THE WAY OUT OF `run`, whether it halted
   normally or failed (`Vm.closeAllFiles`, run from an `errdefer` and
   explicitly on the HALT path) — bounded by `max_open_files` while running,
   since the table has no allocator to grow with.

   `Vm.diagnostic` carries detail a bare Zig error tag can't: the four
   file-related errors that set a path/cause do (`FileOpenFailed`,
   `TooManyOpenFiles`, `FilesUnavailable`, `FileCloseFailed`); the other four
   (`StreamNotReadable`, `StreamNotWritable`, `StreamClosed`,
   `CannotCloseStandardStream`) need none — the bare tag already says
   everything. `main.zig` prefers this diagnostic when printing a runtime
   error, falling back to `@errorName(err)` for any error that doesn't set
   one.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   11. MAPS, LISTS, AND THE HEAP (the VM's only heap-managed value; nine of
       the ten heap-related opcodes are in section 3 — JSON_PARSE is
       section 12's)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `map`/`list` add one runtime value kind — OBJECT (section 2) — and the
   heap/allocator backing it (`Vm` carries a `std.mem.Allocator`, via
   `Vm.init(allocator)`); every compile-time string literal is ALSO an
   OBJECT (section 2), so a program that touches only scalars still never
   allocates, but any string at all does.

   `value.Object` IS A REFCOUNTED HEAP HEADER, NOT A TRACED ONE:
   ```
   Object = struct {
       refcount: usize = 1,
       payload: union(enum) {
           string: []const u8,                       // heap-owned bytes
           list: std.ArrayList(Value),
           map: std.StringArrayHashMapUnmanaged(Value),
       },
   }
   ```
   `refcount` starts at 1 and is adjusted by `Value.incref`/`Value.decref`;
   reaching 0 frees the object, recursively decreffing every child `Value`
   it holds and freeing every map key it owns — a plain recursive free, not
   mark/sweep. A self-referential structure (`m["self"] := m`) never reaches
   refcount 0 and leaks for the life of the `Vm.run` call that built it — a
   known, accepted limitation. `print`ing one is separately guarded by a
   hard recursion-depth cap (64).

   `Value.newString(allocator, bytes)` is the single constructor for a
   string OBJECT — used by literal compilation, map-literal keys,
   `MAP_KEYS`, and JSON parsing alike. A Chunk's constant pool holds one
   permanent reference per OBJECT-backed constant it contains (released on
   `Chunk.deinit`); PUSH_CONST increfs the constant before pushing a copy,
   exactly like LOAD_LOCAL increfs a local before pushing its copy.

   `Value.asStringBytes` reads a string OBJECT's bytes for comparison/
   printing/indexing, regardless of how it was constructed. A list/map, by
   contrast, compares by POINTER IDENTITY ONLY, never deep (a deep
   comparison could hang on the accepted cycle above).

   A MAP IS ORDER-PRESERVING (`std.StringArrayHashMapUnmanaged`, not a plain
   hash map), so `keys(...)`, `print`, and JSON output all see deterministic,
   insertion-order results. Every map KEY is a fresh, map-owned copy
   (`allocator.dupe`'d on first insertion, freed on removal/overwrite/
   destroy) — never aliasing whatever expression produced the key.

   `Local.collection` is a separate axis from `Local.array` (section 5): a
   map/list local is always exactly one heap-reference slot, addressed via
   INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET/LIST_PUSH/MAP_HAS/MAP_DELETE/MAP_KEYS/LEN_VALUE, and
   the VM re-checks the actual runtime tag before every one of those
   (`TypeMismatch` if it doesn't match what the compiler expected).
   `Local.is_string` is a third, independent axis for the same reason: a
   string local is likewise always one heap-reference slot, but addressed
   via INDEX_GET/INDEX_SLICE only — never INDEX_SET (GRAMMAR.bnf's Strings
   design notes), which is why it isn't just folded into `CollectionKind`.

   BRACKET INDEXING IS GENERIC, NOT SLOT-BASED, BECAUSE IT HAS TO CHAIN.
   Unlike LOAD_INDEX/LOAD_INDEX_REF (which take a compile-time-known slot),
   INDEX_GET/INDEX_SET/INDEX_SLICE pop their container off the stack as an
   ordinary `Value`, however it got there — necessary for a chained
   `doc["a"]["b"]`, where the base `doc["a"]` is already a value on the
   stack, not a named local. A bare identifier resolving to neither an
   array, a map/list, nor a string local is a compile-time
   `SemanticError.NotIndexable`; indexing into a value that turns out scalar
   at runtime is `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch`. A bare ARRAY-typed local is
   additionally rejected at compile time for `INDEX_SLICE` specifically (raw
   arrays aren't first-class values to slice — design note 3e), even though
   a single INDEX_GET-style read isn't how a fixed/generic array is indexed
   at all (that's still LOAD_INDEX/LOAD_INDEX_REF, section 6).

   REFCOUNTING: INCREF ON DUPLICATION, DECREF ON DISCARD-WITHOUT-MOVING.
   Both are no-ops for every non-OBJECT value. The rule: incref when a
   `Value` is duplicated into a second live location while the first stays
   live; decref when a live location's `Value` is permanently destroyed or
   overwritten without moving. A pure MOVE (temporarily on the expression
   stack, then relocated to a new permanent home with nothing left behind)
   needs neither — var-decl initializers, MAKE_LIST/MAKE_MAP's element
   transplant, LIST_PUSH's appended value, and RET's copied-down return
   value are all moves. See section 3 for exactly which instructions
   incref/decref. `Vm.run`'s two exit paths (a propagated error, and the
   ordinary HALT path) each decref every value still on the stack in one
   pass, since top-level code (frame 0) never runs a RET of its own.

   A MAP/LIST VALUE NEEDS NONE OF SECTION 6'S ESCAPE ANALYSIS. An ARRAY_REF
   points into the VM's stack, which the returning frame is about to reuse;
   an OBJECT points into the heap, with its own refcounted lifetime
   independent of any stack frame. So a map/list can be returned, stored
   inside another map/list, or handed to a function and returned back, with
   no restriction — it's simply one more ordinary `Value` wherever a scalar
   argument or return would go.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   12. JSON (json/stringify — one opcode each, no new value kind beyond
       section 11's)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `json(buffer, count)` adds JSON_PARSE: it gathers `count` bytes via the
   same `Vm.gatherBytes`/`io_buffer` staging area WRITE_BYTES uses (section
   9), then `src/json.zig` parses them via `std.json.parseFromSlice` into a
   `std.json.Value` tree (a throwaway scratch arena, freed immediately) and
   walks it: a JSON object becomes a map, an array a list, a string a fresh
   string value (`Value.newString`), a number an INT or FLOAT, a boolean
   BOOL, and `null` NULL. Every heap object the walk builds already has the
   refcount a fresh owner expects. `json` is a special form (parsed like
   `len`/`read`/`write`/`open`), not an ordinary `func`, because its result
   type isn't knowable until the bytes are read. Malformed input is
   `RuntimeError.JsonParseFailed`, diagnosed via `Vm.diagnostic` with a fixed
   `cause` string (every parse failure collapses to one error before it
   reaches the VM).

   `stringify(value)` adds JSON_STRINGIFY, the reverse: a recursive walk
   over `Value` (INT/FLOAT as JSON numbers, BOOL as true/false, NULL as
   null, OBJECT's list/map payloads as `[...]`/`{"k":v,...}`) producing a
   fresh string value instead of consuming one. String escaping goes through
   `std.json.Stringify.encodeJsonString` — unlike `Value.print`'s
   human-readable rendering (bare, unescaped, meant for a person to read),
   this properly escapes quote/backslash/control characters so the result
   round-trips through `json(...)`. A stream or array reference has no JSON
   representation: `src/json.zig` reports `error.Unstringifiable`, collapsed
   to `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch` at the JSON_STRINGIFY handler. `stringify`
   is a special form for a different reason than `json` is — its result
   type (`string`) IS fixed, same as any `func`'s declared return type; what
   rules out an ordinary `func` here is that nothing else in the language
   lets a `func` body build a string a piece at a time.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   13. NUMERIC PARSING (int/float call-syntax — one opcode each, no new
       value kind; GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3r)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   PARSE_INT                ( v -- int )      pops v; branches on its
                                               runtime shape (`int(x)` is
                                               ONE opcode covering both of
                                               its forms, GRAMMAR.bnf design
                                               note 3r):
                                                 - STRING: parses its bytes
                                                   as a base-10 signed
                                                   integer literal via
                                                   `std.fmt.parseInt(i64, ..,
                                                   10)`. `RuntimeError.
                                                   NumberParseFailed` (with a
                                                   `Vm.diagnostic`, operation
                                                   "int") for anything
                                                   `parseInt` itself rejects:
                                                   empty input, a non-digit
                                                   byte, surrounding
                                                   whitespace, a decimal
                                                   point, or a magnitude
                                                   outside i64. A leading '+'
                                                   or '-' IS accepted
                                                   (`parseInt`'s own sign
                                                   handling).
                                                 - FLOAT: truncates toward
                                                   zero into an i64 (checked
                                                   first against i64's
                                                   representable range
                                                   rather than trusting
                                                   `@intFromFloat`'s own
                                                   safety-checked-UB
                                                   precondition —
                                                   `Vm.checkedIntFromFloat`).
                                                   `RuntimeError.Overflow`
                                                   (the same variant ADD/SUB/
                                                   MUL already use) for NaN,
                                                   ±Infinity, or any
                                                   magnitude whose integer
                                                   part doesn't fit an i64.
                                                 - Anything else (INT
                                                   included — no implicit
                                                   "already an int"
                                                   identity): TypeMismatch.
                                               Pushes the result as an INT.
                                               What `int(x)` (GRAMMAR.bnf
                                               design note 3r) compiles to.
   PARSE_FLOAT               ( v -- float )   pops v (must be string-shaped;
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise);
                                               parses its bytes as a
                                               floating-point literal via
                                               `std.fmt.parseFloat(f64,
                                               ..)`, pushing the result as a
                                               FLOAT. `RuntimeError.
                                               NumberParseFailed` (with a
                                               `Vm.diagnostic`, operation
                                               "float") for anything
                                               `parseFloat` itself rejects
                                               (empty input, a non-numeric
                                               byte, surrounding
                                               whitespace). What `float(s)`
                                               compiles to. Unlike PARSE_INT,
                                               a plain integer-shaped string
                                               ("42") is valid input here
                                               too, and so is "nan"/"inf"/
                                               "-inf" (`parseFloat`'s own
                                               special-value recognition,
                                               case-insensitive).

   Both are ordinary special forms parsed like JSON/STRINGIFY (section 12) —
   `int`/`float` are also <type> keywords (section 2's VALUE REPRESENTATION
   note doesn't change), but the parser never confuses the two: a <type> is
   only ever consulted at the very start of a var-declaration/param/
   return-type position, never while parsing an <expression> (GRAMMAR.bnf
   design note 3r). Neither opcode touches the heap or any refcount beyond
   decreffing its own popped string operand (`Vm.pop`'s usual convention) —
   the pushed result is a plain scalar INT/FLOAT either way.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   14. ERROR HANDLING (PUSH_HANDLER, POP_HANDLER — section 3; one new runtime
       table; no new value kind — the binding is an ordinary RECORD, section
       19)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   What `try <block> catch IDENTIFIER <block>` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3u)
   lowers to. The codegen pattern is in section 4; this section is the
   runtime half.

   THE HANDLER STACK is a fixed array of 64 entries beside the call frames
   (vm.zig's `Exec`), sized like the open-file table (section 10) and for
   the same reason: the VM has no allocator at run time. Each entry records
   the chunk, the catch block's entry offset in it, sp, bp, frame count, and
   return width. Exceeding 64 live handlers is `HandlerStackOverflow`.

   The limit has to be a RUNTIME one rather than a compile-time one: lexical
   `try` nesting within a chunk is bounded and countable, but recursion
   through a function that itself contains a `try` is not.

   UNWINDING. When the dispatch loop sees a CATCHABLE error (below) and the
   handler stack is non-empty, it takes the innermost handler H and:

     1. pops H;
     2. decrefs stack[H.sp .. sp] and sets sp = H.sp — the same loop that
        already runs over the whole stack at HALT/EXIT, just bounded below;
        correct across frames for the same reason it is there, since the
        physical stack is one flat array;
     3. restores chunk, bp, frame count and return width from H, and sets
        ip = H.ip;
     4. clears `Vm.diagnostic`, so a later detail-free uncaught error can't
        report stale detail from an earlier caught one;
     5. builds the error struct and pushes it — landing it at exactly the
        slot the compiler allocated for the binding (section 4);
     6. continues the loop.

   Note what is NOT restored: the open-file table. A stream can legitimately
   outlive the block that opened it, and the VM cannot tell that case apart
   from one that can't (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3u). Every still-open file is
   flushed and closed at HALT/EXIT as always.

   THE ERROR STRUCT is an ordinary heap RECORD (section 19), an instance of
   the built-in `Error` type (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3z), with four fields,
   always all present, in this fixed order: "error" (the RuntimeError tag
   name, via `@errorName`), "message" (rendered exactly the way the CLI
   renders an uncaught error, so a caught-and-reported one reads
   identically), and "operation"/"path" from `Vm.diagnostic`, `""` when
   there is none. No new value kind is involved — `Vm.errorValue` builds it
   directly (not through MAKE_STRUCT, which only ever runs for a `{...}`
   struct literal in compiled source), but the resulting `Object` is
   indistinguishable from one MAKE_STRUCT would have built, so ordinary
   FIELD_GET reads it. `Vm.errorValue`'s field order and
   `Compiler.compileModules`'s registration of the `Error` type (pass 0)
   must stay in exact sync — the former hardcodes the field indices the
   latter's declaration order fixes.

   CATCHABLE vs NOT. Twenty-seven RuntimeError variants are catchable:
   TypeMismatch, DivisionByZero, Overflow, IndexOutOfBounds, ByteOutOfRange,
   StreamReadFailed, StreamWriteFailed, FileOpenFailed, TooManyOpenFiles,
   FileCloseFailed, StreamNotReadable, StreamNotWritable, StreamClosed,
   CannotCloseStandardStream, FilesUnavailable, KeyNotFound, JsonParseFailed,
   NumberParseFailed, InvalidExitCode, ListDirFailed, RemoveFailed,
   RenameFailed (section 16), ProcessesUnavailable, ProcessSpawnFailed,
   ProcessTerminatedAbnormally (section 17), ClockUnavailable, and
   InvalidRange (section 18).

   Four are not: StackOverflow, StackUnderflow, CallStackOverflow, and
   HandlerStackOverflow. These are VM-integrity failures rather than program
   conditions, and running a catch block requires the very stack room that
   has just been exhausted. Neither is `error.OutOfMemory` — it is not a
   RuntimeError at all (it comes from the Zig allocator), and building the
   error struct itself allocates, so a handler for it could not run. The
   catchable set is enumerated explicitly in vm.zig rather than tested by
   exclusion, so adding a RuntimeError forces a decision about it.

   EXIT is not in either list: it is not an error. It returns from `Vm.run`
   normally (section 3's EXIT entry), which bypasses the handler stack with
   no special case — `exit` inside a try block still halts the program.

   RET DROPS STALE HANDLERS. A `return` from inside a try block would
   otherwise leave that frame's handler installed with an ip into a chunk
   that is no longer running. RET therefore pops every handler whose
   recorded frame count exceeds the post-return frame count. Doing this at
   run time rather than making the compiler emit a POP_HANDLER before each
   in-try `return` keeps it correct for every path out of a frame by
   construction. Butter has no `break` or `continue`, so `return`, `exit`,
   and falling off the end are the only ways to leave a try block other than
   the error path itself, and the latter two need nothing.

   A stale or spent handler is worth testing for deliberately, because it is
   NOT observable through the reported error alone: a failure that lands in a
   dead catch block usually re-raises or re-reaches the same condition and
   reports the identical error. Detecting it takes a side effect inside the
   handler that should never run.

   COST WHEN UNUSED is one comparison per RET and nothing else — a program
   containing no `try` is emitted byte-for-byte as it was before this
   feature existed, and the benchmarks are unchanged.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   15. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES (GET_ENV, HAS_ENV — one opcode each, no new
       value kind, no new runtime table; GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3v)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   GET_ENV                  ( name -- string )  pops name (must be
                                               string-shaped; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise), looks it up in
                                               `Host.env` (vm.zig) and pushes
                                               its value as a FRESH heap
                                               string (section 11). A name
                                               that isn't set pushes `""` —
                                               NOT an error, and not NULL, so
                                               this opcode's result is
                                               unconditionally a string and
                                               the compiler can type it as
                                               one. What `getenv(name)`
                                               compiles to.
   HAS_ENV                  ( name -- bool )   pops name (same TypeMismatch
                                               rule), pushes whether it is
                                               SET. The only way to tell a
                                               variable set to the empty
                                               string from one that doesn't
                                               exist, since GET_ENV reads
                                               both as `""` — the same split
                                               MAP_HAS gives a map (section
                                               11). What `hasenv(name)`
                                               compiles to.

   NEITHER TAKES AN OPERAND, for the reason PUSH_ARGS doesn't: the
   environment is live `Vm.run` state rather than anything in the chunk, so
   there is nothing for the compiler to fold. The NAME is likewise an
   ordinary popped value, not a constant-pool index, which is what lets it
   be computed (`getenv("BUTTER_" + setting)`).

   `Host.env` IS A FLAT SLICE of name/value pairs, not a hash map, and
   lookup is a linear scan. A program reads a handful of variables at
   startup, never one per loop iteration the way it reads a stack slot, so
   the constant factor is irrelevant and this keeps the whole `Host` plain
   injected data. A repeated name resolves to its FIRST entry. Comparison is
   byte-exact on every platform, Windows included (GRAMMAR.bnf design note
   3v explains why).

   REFCOUNTS. Each pops and decrefs its own name operand (`Vm.pop`'s usual
   convention, section 14's addendum). GET_ENV's result is a fresh copy of
   the value, never a slice aliasing `Host.env`'s own storage — the same
   guarantee PUSH_ARGS's strings carry, and what lets the returned string
   outlive the slice the embedder passed in. HAS_ENV touches the heap not at
   all: its result is a plain scalar BOOL.

   NOT A CAPABILITY GATE, unlike `Host.fs` (section 10). An absent
   `Host.env` is simply an EMPTY environment — every variable unset — rather
   than a refusal like FilesUnavailable, because a program cannot tell the
   two apart and there is nothing to refuse: reading a variable that isn't
   there has no effect outside the VM. There is no SET_ENV counterpart;
   these two opcodes are read-only, and the environment cannot be enumerated.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   16. DIRECTORY AND FILESYSTEM METADATA (PATH_EXISTS, LIST_DIR, PATH_REMOVE,
       PATH_RENAME — one opcode each, no new value kind, reuses section 10's
       `Host.fs`; GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3w)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   PATH_EXISTS               ( path -- bool )  pops path (must be
                                               string-shaped; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise). `RuntimeError.
                                               FilesUnavailable` if `Host.fs`
                                               is null (the same capability
                                               gate OPEN uses, section 10);
                                               otherwise checks `path`
                                               against `fs.dir` and pushes
                                               whether it names anything
                                               right now. UNLIKE every other
                                               opcode this section defines,
                                               this one has no third failure
                                               mode: any `fs.dir.access`
                                               error OTHER than the two above
                                               (not found, no permission, a
                                               bad path, ...) is swallowed and
                                               reported as `false` rather
                                               than propagated — deliberately
                                               lenient, matching the
                                               inherently advisory,
                                               time-of-check-to-time-of-use-
                                               racy nature of an existence
                                               check on any real filesystem
                                               (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3w).
                                               What `exists(path)` compiles
                                               to.
   LIST_DIR                  ( path -- list )  pops path (same TypeMismatch
                                               rule and FilesUnavailable
                                               gate as PATH_EXISTS). Opens
                                               `path` against `fs.dir` with
                                               iteration enabled and walks
                                               it ONE LEVEL (not recursive),
                                               pushing a FRESH list Object
                                               (section 11) of FRESH heap
                                               strings (`Value.newString`),
                                               one per entry NAME only (no
                                               kind, no path, just the bare
                                               name a second `open`/`exists`
                                               call could append to `path`
                                               itself), in whatever order the
                                               OS iterator hands them back —
                                               never sorted, never `"."` or
                                               `".."`. Opening `path` as an
                                               iterable directory, or an
                                               error partway through the
                                               walk, is `RuntimeError.
                                               ListDirFailed` (with a
                                               `Vm.diagnostic`: operation
                                               `"listDir"`, the path, and
                                               `@errorName` of whatever the
                                               host filesystem reported) —
                                               UNLIKE PATH_EXISTS, this has
                                               no lenient "couldn't tell,
                                               call it false" fallback: there
                                               is no empty-list reading of
                                               "I couldn't list this" that
                                               wouldn't be misleading. The
                                               directory handle is closed
                                               before this instruction
                                               returns, success or failure,
                                               so a program that calls
                                               `listDir` in a loop can't
                                               exhaust any handle table over
                                               it. What `listDir(path)`
                                               compiles to.
   PATH_REMOVE                ( path -- bool ) pops path (same TypeMismatch
                                               rule and FilesUnavailable gate
                                               as PATH_EXISTS). Tries to
                                               delete `path` as a file first;
                                               if the host reports it's
                                               actually a directory, retries
                                               as an (empty-only) directory
                                               delete. `path` not existing —
                                               from EITHER attempt — pushes
                                               `false`, not an error: the
                                               same "absent is a no-op" split
                                               MAP_DELETE (section 11) gives
                                               a map key, extended to the
                                               filesystem. Any OTHER failure
                                               (no permission, a NON-EMPTY
                                               directory, an I/O error) is
                                               `RuntimeError.RemoveFailed`
                                               (with a `Vm.diagnostic`:
                                               operation `"remove"`, the
                                               path, and the underlying
                                               cause) — on success, pushes
                                               `true`. There is no recursive
                                               "remove a directory and
                                               everything in it" here; a
                                               program that wants that empties
                                               the directory itself first (via
                                               LIST_DIR + one PATH_REMOVE per
                                               entry). What `remove(path)`
                                               compiles to.
   PATH_RENAME    ( from to -- bool )          pops to, then from (both must
                                               be string-shaped; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise, checked independently
                                               so a bad `to` doesn't mask a
                                               bad `from` or vice versa — see
                                               vm.zig's `renamePath`), same
                                               FilesUnavailable gate as the
                                               other three (diagnosed against
                                               `from`). FIRST checks whether
                                               `from` exists via an
                                               independent `fs.dir.access`
                                               call — NOT by inspecting the
                                               rename attempt's own error,
                                               because on at least one
                                               supported host `error.
                                               FileNotFound` from the rename
                                               itself can't be told apart
                                               from `to`'s PARENT directory
                                               being missing, a genuine
                                               failure rather than a no-op.
                                               If `from` doesn't exist,
                                               pushes `false` immediately
                                               (same "absent is a no-op"
                                               split as PATH_REMOVE) without
                                               attempting the rename at all.
                                               Otherwise renames `from` to
                                               `to` against `fs.dir`
                                               (replacing `to` if it already
                                               exists, the underlying OS
                                               rename's own behavior); any
                                               failure at THIS point — no
                                               permission, `to`'s parent
                                               missing, a cross-device move,
                                               an I/O error — is
                                               `RuntimeError.RenameFailed`
                                               (with a `Vm.diagnostic`:
                                               operation `"rename"`, `from`
                                               as the path, and the
                                               underlying cause), never
                                               folded into the `false` case.
                                               Pushes `true` on success. What
                                               `rename(from, to)` compiles
                                               to.

   NONE OF THE FOUR TAKES AN OPERAND, for the same reason GET_ENV/HAS_ENV
   don't (section 15): every path is an ordinary popped `Value`, not a
   compile-time constant, so a computed path
   (`remove(dir + "/" + name)`) works with no special codegen. REFCOUNTS
   follow `Vm.pop`'s usual convention (section 14's addendum): each pops and
   decrefs every path operand it's handed; PATH_EXISTS/PATH_REMOVE/
   PATH_RENAME's results are plain scalar BOOLs (nothing to incref);
   LIST_DIR's result is a fresh heap Object nobody else holds a reference to,
   exactly like MAP_KEYS's (section 11).

   REUSES `Host.fs` (section 10) RATHER THAN ADDING A SECOND CAPABILITY GATE.
   A program that can `open` a file can already reach everything these four
   opcodes need — `fs.dir` is the same `std.Io.Dir` OPEN resolves a relative
   path against — so gating them separately would only mean an embedder that
   grants file access has to remember to grant this too, for no added
   safety. `Host.fs` null still means the same thing it always has:
   `RuntimeError.FilesUnavailable` for all four, exactly like OPEN.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   17. SUBPROCESS EXECUTION (EXEC — one new opcode, no new value kind beyond
       section 11's `map`, one new runtime capability gate `Host.process`;
       GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3x)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   EXEC has its full stack-effect entry in section 3; this section is the
   capability-gate and Host-wiring detail, the same split section 10 (Files)
   and section 16 (Directory/filesystem metadata) give OPEN and
   PATH_EXISTS/LIST_DIR/PATH_REMOVE/PATH_RENAME respectively.

   `Host.process: ?Process = null` (vm.zig) — `Process` is `{ io: std.Io,
   dir: std.Io.Dir }`, the EXACT SAME SHAPE as `Host.Fs` (section 10). This
   is deliberate, not incidental: an embedder (or a test) that wants `exec`'s
   spawned children and `open`/`exists`/`listDir`/... to agree on "the
   current directory" passes the SAME `dir` value to both `Host.fs` and
   `Host.process`. Unlike every other capability this VM injects (`args`,
   `env`), `process` defaults to ABSENT rather than "present but empty" or
   "present but inert" — a spawned child can do anything the embedding
   process itself could (read/write any file the OS lets it, open network
   connections, spawn further children of its own), entirely unbounded by
   whatever `Host.fs`/`Host.process.dir` scoping the embedder set up for
   THIS Vm's own `open`/`exists`/etc. — so granting it is a strictly bigger
   step than granting filesystem access, and gets its own opt-in rather than
   riding along on `Host.fs` the way section 16's four opcodes deliberately
   do (their own "REUSES Host.fs" note, above, doesn't apply here).

   `Vm.execProcess` (vm.zig) implements EXEC:

     1. `host.process orelse` -> `RuntimeError.ProcessesUnavailable`, with a
        `Vm.diagnostic` (operation "exec", path = command, same shape every
        other capability-gate failure in this VM carries).
     2. Builds argv as `[command] ++ args` (`args`'s elements, gathered via
        `Value.asStringBytes` — `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch` on the first one
        that isn't string-shaped, checked lazily as each is visited rather
        than in one up-front pass).
     3. `std.process.run(self.allocator, process.io, .{ .argv = argv, .cwd =
        .{ .dir = process.dir } })` — spawns the child with its OWN
        `stdin = .ignore` (there is no way, in this pass, to pipe bytes INTO
        a spawned child) and `stdout`/`stderr` both piped and fully
        captured, unlimited, only once the child has actually exited (there
        is no incremental/streaming form). A spawn failure (no such program,
        no permission, ...) is `RuntimeError.ProcessSpawnFailed`, diagnosed
        the same way.
     4. The child's `std.process.Child.Term`: `.exited => |code|` becomes
        `"exit_code"` directly (already a `u8`, so always in 0..255 — no
        separate range check is needed the way EXIT's own operand needs
        one, since this value came FROM a real process exit, not arbitrary
        Butter source). Any other `Term` (`.signal`, `.stopped`, `.unknown`
        — the child was killed, suspended, or terminated some other way
        that isn't a normal exit) is `RuntimeError.ProcessTerminatedAbnormally`
        instead: freeing the already-captured stdout/stderr buffers first
        (`std.process.run` still allocates and returns them even for a
        non-`.exited` term), since there is no sane `int` to report as
        `"exit_code"` for a process that never "exited" in the ordinary
        sense.
     5. On success, builds a fresh `map` (section 11) with exactly three
        keys, always all present: `"stdout"`/`"stderr"` — ADOPTING
        `std.process.run`'s own returned buffers directly as each string
        Object's owned bytes (`Vm.mapSetOwnedString`) rather than duplicating
        them the way `Value.newString` does, since nothing else holds a
        reference to them — and `"exit_code"` (a plain scalar `int`, nothing
        to incref).

   THE CHILD'S ENVIRONMENT IS NOT `Host.env` (section 15). `Host.env` only
   governs what THIS Butter program's OWN `getenv`/`hasenv` calls see; EXEC
   passes `environ_map = null` to `std.process.run`, meaning the spawned
   child inherits this actual OS process's REAL environment whole, the same
   way any ordinary shelled-out command from any other language would. An
   embedder cannot use `Host.env` to sandbox what a child process itself
   observes — only `Host.process` (present or absent) controls whether one
   can be spawned at all.

   REFCOUNTS. EXEC pops and decrefs both operands (`args` first, since it was
   pushed last — `Vm.pop`'s usual convention, section 14's addendum); each
   `args` element is only ever READ (`asStringBytes`), never consumed, since
   the popped `args` list value (and everything it owns) is decreffed as a
   whole once `execProcess` returns, success or failure. The pushed result
   map is a fresh heap Object nobody else holds a reference to, exactly like
   LIST_DIR's own result (section 16).
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   18. TIME AND RANDOMNESS (NOW, RANDOM_FLOAT, RANDOM_RANGE — three new
       opcodes, no new value kind, one new runtime capability gate
       `Host.clock` plus a non-gating `Host.rng_seed` override; GRAMMAR.bnf
       design note 3y)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   NOW                       ( -- float )       `host.clock orelse` ->
                                               `RuntimeError.ClockUnavailable`
                                               (with a `Vm.diagnostic`:
                                               operation "now", same shape
                                               every other capability-gate
                                               failure in this VM carries).
                                               Otherwise reads
                                               `std.Io.Clock.real.now(io)`
                                               (wall-clock time, POSIX/Unix
                                               epoch — `std.Io`'s own `Clock.
                                               real` doc comment), split into
                                               whole seconds and a fractional
                                               nanosecond remainder BEFORE
                                               converting either half to
                                               `f64` (see below for why), and
                                               pushes their sum as a FLOAT.
                                               What `now()` compiles to.
   RANDOM_FLOAT               ( -- float )      `Vm.randomInterface` (below)
                                               for a generator, then pushes
                                               `r.float(f64)` — uniformly
                                               distributed in `[0, 1)`,
                                               `std.Random.float`'s own
                                               documented range. What
                                               `random()` (no arguments)
                                               compiles to.
   RANDOM_RANGE   ( start end -- int )          pops end, then start (both
                                               must be INT; TypeMismatch
                                               otherwise, decreffing
                                               whichever operand(s) are heap
                                               objects — e.g. a STRING passed
                                               where an INT was expected, the
                                               same accounting `popNumericPair`
                                               already does). `RuntimeError.
                                               InvalidRange` if `start >=
                                               end` — CHECKED BEFORE calling
                                               `Vm.randomInterface`, so an
                                               invalid range never draws any
                                               entropy or needs `Host.clock`/
                                               `rng_seed` at all. Otherwise
                                               gets a generator the same way
                                               RANDOM_FLOAT does and pushes
                                               `r.intRangeLessThan(i64, start,
                                               end)` as an INT — end
                                               EXCLUSIVE, mirroring the
                                               for-loop's own `start..end`
                                               (section 7). What `random(start,
                                               end)` compiles to.

   NONE OF THE THREE TAKES AN OPERAND. NOW needs none for the same reason
   GET_ENV/HAS_ENV don't (section 15): the clock is live `Vm.run` state, not
   anything in the chunk. RANDOM_RANGE's `start`/`end` are ordinary popped
   values instead, exactly like PATH_RENAME's `from`/`to` (section 16) — a
   computed range (`random(lo, lo + n)`) works with no special codegen.

   WHY NOW SPLITS SECONDS FROM NANOSECONDS BEFORE CONVERTING. `Io.Timestamp.
   nanoseconds` is an `i96` — nanoseconds since the epoch is already past
   `f64`'s ~53-bit exact-integer range (roughly 1.7e18 against a ~9.007e15
   ceiling), so a direct `@floatFromInt(ns) / 1e9` would round away exactly
   the sub-second precision this opcode exists to provide. Splitting into
   `whole_seconds = @divTrunc(ns, ns_per_s)` (~1.7e9, comfortably exact as an
   `f64`) and `frac_ns = @mod(ns, ns_per_s)` (always < 1e9, likewise exact)
   BEFORE converting either half keeps the result accurate to the underlying
   clock's own resolution instead of merely to the nearest whole second.

   `Vm.randomInterface(host)` — the seeding logic RANDOM_FLOAT/RANDOM_RANGE
   share:

     1. If already seeded this `run` (`Vm.prng_seeded`), returns
        `self.prng.random()` directly — no re-seeding, so a program that
        calls `random`/`random(...)` more than once gets a genuine SEQUENCE
        of values, not the same one repeated.
     2. Otherwise, `host.rng_seed` WINS IF SET: seeds `std.Random.
        DefaultPrng.init(seed)` directly from it, entirely bypassing
        `host.clock` — a seeded program never touches clock access at all,
        even if the embedder also granted it.
     3. Otherwise, `host.clock orelse` -> the SAME `RuntimeError.
        ClockUnavailable` NOW raises (with `Vm.diagnostic`'s operation set
        to "random" instead of "now", the only difference between the two
        capability-gate failures); if present, draws 8 bytes via `io.
        random(&buf)` (`std.Io`'s own fast, non-blocking OS-entropy source —
        NOT `io.randomSecure`, since a seed for a general-purpose PRNG has
        no need for cryptographic strength) and seeds `DefaultPrng` from
        those, interpreted as a little-endian `u64`.
     4. Either way, marks `Vm.prng_seeded = true` and caches the seeded
        generator in `Vm.prng` (a `std.Random.DefaultPrng`, a field of `Vm`
        itself — this VM has no allocator-free-at-runtime constraint the way
        the fixed open-file/handler tables do, section 10/14, but the
        seed-once-per-run shape is the same idea) for the rest of this
        `run`.

   `Host.clock: ?std.Io = null` (vm.zig) — gated exactly like `Host.fs`
   (section 10) and `Host.process` (section 17): `null` is a refusal, not
   "present but inert". Unlike THOSE two, though, this is not a "the program
   could do real damage" gate — reading the clock or drawing entropy has no
   side effect reaching outside the VM the way a file write or a spawned
   process does. It is a capability gate anyway because Zig's own wall-clock
   and OS-entropy access both go through `std.Io` in this compiler version
   (`std.Io.Clock.real.now`/`Io.random`) rather than a global ambient
   function the way an older Zig's `std.time.timestamp`/`std.crypto.random`
   were — so `Host.clock` is what an embedder hands over to make either
   builtin work AT ALL, the same reason `Host.fs`/`Host.process` exist,
   even though the THREAT MODEL motivating each is different.

   `Host.rng_seed: ?u64 = null` (vm.zig) is NOT a capability gate — it is an
   override, checked before `Host.clock` is even consulted (`randomInterface`
   step 2, above). This is the deterministic test mode this feature's own
   TODO entry called for: a test (or an embedder that wants reproducible
   output for its own reasons) sets a fixed seed and gets EXACTLY the same
   sequence of `random`/`random(...)` results every run, with no `Host.clock`
   needed at all. `now()` has no equivalent override — pinning wall-clock
   time to an arbitrary fixed value would defeat its own purpose, so its own
   tests instead assert PLAUSIBILITY (a real clock, e.g. `std.testing.io`,
   checked against a wide sane range) rather than an exact value, and
   integration coverage (tests/cases/time_random.butter) reaches it only
   through its OWN capability gate (no `Host.clock` granted, `try`/`catch`
   around it) rather than a real timestamp.

   REFCOUNTS. NOW pops nothing (nothing to decref) and pushes a plain scalar
   FLOAT (nothing to incref). RANDOM_FLOAT likewise pushes a plain scalar
   FLOAT. RANDOM_RANGE pops two plain scalar INTs on the success path (no
   refcounting) but must still decref whichever operand(s) are heap objects
   on the TypeMismatch path (`Vm.pop`'s usual convention, section 14's
   addendum) — the one place in this section refcounting is actually live,
   since a non-INT `start`/`end` could be a STRING.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   19. RECORDS (MAKE_STRUCT, FIELD_GET, FIELD_SET — three new opcodes,
       section 3; one new OBJECT payload, RECORD; one new `Program`-level
       table, struct_types; GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3z)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   RECORD (a fourth `value.Object.Payload` variant, alongside STRING/list/
   map) is `{ type_name: []const u8, field_names: []const []const u8,
   fields: []Value }`. `type_name`/`field_names` are shared, PROGRAM-owned
   slices — every instance of the same struct type points at the exact same
   two slices (borrowed from that type's own `chunk_mod.StructType` entry,
   below), never duplicated per instance; only `fields` (this instance's own
   values) is unique and owned by this object. A struct value is a genuine
   heap OBJECT, refcounted exactly like a list/map — `b := a` where `a` is
   struct-typed aliases the same object, mutation through one is visible
   through the other — but unlike a map, field access never hashes a
   string: `fields[i]` is always the value of the i-th field IN DECLARATION
   ORDER, an index resolved once, at compile time, from the field's name.

   `chunk_mod.Program.struct_types: []const StructType` — a sibling table to
   `Program.functions`, `StructType = { type_name, field_names }` — one
   entry per struct OR enum type registered across the whole compiled
   program (compiler.zig's `Compiler.types`), indexed identically: an
   enum's own entry exists (`field_names = &.{}`) but is never actually
   read, since MAKE_STRUCT's operand, by construction, only ever names a
   struct's own type_index — this keeps a type_index resolved anywhere in
   the compiler valid here unchanged, with no separate struct-only
   renumbering pass needed. Index 0 is always the built-in `Error` type
   (section 14, GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3u) — `Compiler.compileModules`
   registers it before any user struct/enum specifically to fix that index,
   which `Vm.errorValue` hardcodes rather than looking up by name.

   MAKE_STRUCT/FIELD_GET/FIELD_SET are documented in full in section 3,
   above. The key structural fact those three entries lean on: NEITHER
   FIELD_GET NOR FIELD_SET CAN RAISE A RUNTIME ERROR. This is the entire
   payoff of a struct being a NEW, compile-time-resolved value kind rather
   than sugar over `map`: every field name is checked exactly once, at
   compile time (`SemanticError.UnknownField`/`NotAStruct`), so by the time
   either opcode runs, the field is known to exist at that exact index —
   there is no `RuntimeError.KeyNotFound` counterpart the way a bad map key
   read has.

   `Local.named_ref`/`FunctionInfo.return_named_ref`/`param_named_refs`
   (section 5) are how the compiler tracks "this local/parameter/return is
   struct-typed, and which struct" without ever changing `Local.value_type`/
   `ast.ValueType` from a plain closed enum into a payload-carrying union —
   `ast.ValueType.named` is the one new tag; the actual registered-type
   index (and whether it's a struct or an enum) always travels in a sibling
   `?NamedTypeRef{ index, is_enum }` field instead. `compiler.zig`'s
   `Compiler.types: []TypeDecl` is the single source of truth this all
   resolves against, built by a two-stage pre-pass in `compileModules`
   BEFORE functions are even registered (a param/return type may itself
   name a struct/enum) — stage one collects every struct/enum NAME across
   every module (so a struct's own fields can forward/mutually reference
   another type regardless of declaration order, the same freedom functions
   already have calling each other), stage two resolves each struct's field
   types now that every name is known.

   BOTH A STRUCT LITERAL (`TypeName{field: expr, ...}`) AND A FIELD
   READ/WRITE (`p.field`, `p.field := v`) ARE FULLY STATIC. A struct literal
   requires EXACTLY the declared field set — no fewer (`MissingField`), no
   more (`UnknownField`), no duplicates (`DuplicateFieldInLiteral`) — and
   compiles each field's value in the struct's OWN declared order regardless
   of the order the literal listed them in, so MAKE_STRUCT's popped values
   always land in the same order `struct_types[type_index].field_names`
   records. A field read/write's base expression must have a STATICALLY
   determinable struct type (`compiler.zig`'s `resolveStructBase`, via the
   same `inferType` the rest of the static type checker, section 5's
   addendum/GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3t, already uses) — `NotAStruct`
   otherwise, even where the base's ACTUAL runtime type might have worked
   out fine (e.g. a map/list element holding a struct) — this is a
   deliberate, permanent restriction, not a "not yet implemented": there is
   no slot to resolve FIELD_GET/FIELD_SET's operand against without a known
   struct type at compile time.

   A struct-typed local/field's DEFAULT value (no initializer; a function
   falling off its end with a struct return type) is never a compile-time
   constant — like an empty map/list, it is a genuine heap allocation, built
   by `compiler.zig`'s `compileStructDefault` emitting each field's own
   default (recursively — a struct field that is itself another struct
   gets its own fresh MAKE_STRUCT) followed by MAKE_STRUCT.

   `==`/`!=` between two struct values are POINTER IDENTITY ONLY, never
   deep — the exact same rule list/map already follow (section 11); no
   special case is added for structs.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   20. ENUMS (no new opcode — an enum variant reference is a PUSH_CONST of
       an ENUM_TAG constant, section 2; GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3aa)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `EnumName.Variant` (e.g. `Color.Red`) compiles to an ordinary PUSH_CONST
   referencing a fresh ENUM_TAG constant added to the CURRENT CHUNK's own
   constant pool — exactly like an INT or STRING literal, and for the same
   reason: it's a value fully known at compile time, so there's nothing for
   a dedicated opcode to compute at run time. `type_name`/`variant_name`
   inside that constant simply borrow the declaration's own source text
   (every other AST-borrowed string in this codebase already assumes the
   source buffer outlives the `Vm.run` call, per ast.zig's file-level doc
   comment — an ENUM_TAG constant needs nothing extra here).

   DISAMBIGUATING A FIELD READ FROM A VARIANT REFERENCE IS THE COMPILER'S
   JOB, NOT THE PARSER'S. The parser produces exactly one node shape,
   `ast.Expr.field_access { base, field }`, for every `<expr> '.' IDENTIFIER`
   — the same way `ast.Expr.Index` doesn't care whether its base is a list
   or a map. `compiler.zig`'s `compileFieldAccess` resolves it: if `base` is
   a bare `.variable` that names NO local currently in scope, but DOES name
   a declared enum type (`Compiler.findTypeIndex`), it's a variant reference
   (`compileEnumVariant`) — a local always wins over a same-named enum type
   if one is in scope. Any other shape must resolve to a struct field read
   (section 19); `SemanticError.NotAStruct`/`UnknownEnumVariant` cover the
   ways either path can fail, both at compile time only.

   AN ENUM-TYPED LOCAL/PARAMETER/RETURN IS TRACKED EXACTLY LIKE A
   STRUCT-TYPED ONE (section 19's `Local.named_ref`/`NamedTypeRef` addendum
   to section 5) — the only difference is `NamedTypeRef.is_enum`, which
   `compiler.zig`'s `StaticType`/`DeclaredType` machinery checks wherever it
   already checks `is_enum` for a struct: `typeCompatible` requires the
   SAME declared enum type, no coercion, not even to/from a plain `int` —
   this is the entire reason enum alternative B (a real distinct value kind)
   was chosen over a bare "named int constants" scheme: `SemanticError.
   TypeMismatch` catches a raw `int` passed where an enum is expected, at
   compile time, the same way a `string` passed where a `bool` is expected
   already is.

   AN ENUM-TYPED LOCAL'S DEFAULT VALUE (no initializer, or falling off a
   function's end) is its FIRST-DECLARED variant (index 0) — an ordinary
   PUSH_CONST, exactly like any other scalar's default, since (unlike a
   struct) an enum value is never a heap allocation.

   `==`/`!=` BETWEEN TWO ENUM_TAG VALUES compare `type_index` AND `variant`
   only (`Value.eql`'s `.enum_value` arm) — true only for the exact same
   declared enum type and the exact same variant; comparing across two
   different enum types, or against a bare INT, is simply `false`, never a
   `RuntimeError` or a `SemanticError` — consistent with GRAMMAR.bnf design
   note 3t's blanket "`==`/`!=` are never statically checked" rule. This is
   a deliberate asymmetry: EVERY OTHER checked position (a var-decl
   initializer, an assignment, a parameter, a return) enforces the exact
   enum-type match at compile time, but `==`/`!=` themselves get no special
   carve-out from the rule every other type pair already follows.

   PRINT rendering (`Value.printAt`'s `.enum_value` arm) is the bare
   `variant_name` (`Red`), unquoted — an ENUM_TAG is self-describing, unlike
   every other case in `printAt` needing either a fixed per-kind string or a
   lookup into the value it's rendering. `json.zig`'s `stringify` reports
   `error.Unstringifiable` for an ENUM_TAG (`write`'s `.enum_value` arm) —
   the same bucket a STREAM or ARRAY_REF falls into (section 12): JSON has
   no native shape for an enum variant, and unlike a struct/map/list there
   is no reasonable one-way rendering either (a bare string would silently
   collide with an ordinary string value). `json_parse`/`convert` needs no
   change in either direction — nothing on the JSON side can produce a
   RECORD or an ENUM_TAG, so `json(...)` can never hand either kind back to
   a program.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   21. CHARACTER CONVERSION (ORD — one new opcode, no new value kind;
       GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3ab)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   ORD                    ( v -- int )        pops v (must be string-shaped;
                                               `RuntimeError.TypeMismatch`
                                               otherwise); if its byte length
                                               isn't exactly 1,
                                               `RuntimeError.
                                               InvalidCharLength` (with a
                                               `Vm.diagnostic`, operation
                                               "ord"); otherwise pushes its
                                               one byte's value (0..255) as
                                               an INT. What `ord(x)`
                                               (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3ab)
                                               compiles to.

   No operand: like PARSE_INT/PARSE_FLOAT (section 13), the string to
   convert is an ordinary popped `Value`, not a compile-time constant.
   Touches no heap state and needs no refcount bookkeeping beyond the
   ordinary decref of its own (string) operand — the result is a plain
   INT, exactly like PARSE_INT's string branch.

   NO SEPARATE `char` VALUE KIND. This deliberately extends the existing
   "a character is a length-1 string" convention (section 11's Strings
   addendum: `s[i]` already returns a fresh length-1 STRING, not a
   dedicated character type) rather than introducing one — ORD is simply
   the byte-level escape hatch that convention was missing, for code that
   needs the numeric value (case conversion, character-class predicates)
   rather than something to print or compare against a string literal.

   BYTE-VALUED, NOT CODEPOINT-VALUED, matching every other byte-oriented
   operation this VM already has (`s[i]`, `s[a..b]`, `read`/`write`'s `int`
   buffers): a lone UTF-8 continuation byte of a multi-byte character is
   still exactly one byte, so it's still a valid ORD operand — the result
   is that raw byte, not a decoded codepoint.

   ONLY THE CHARACTER-TO-BYTE DIRECTION IS PROVIDED HERE — no CHR/byte-to-
   character counterpart opcode exists in this pass, the same "one
   direction only" scope PARSE_INT's own float-to-int (not int-to-float)
   cast already takes (section 13).
   ========================================================================== *)
